module fixtures.free_call_pool_binding.closure_without_definer

// ARM A -- ACCEPTED. Nothing in this closure imports `definer_module`; the compiler adds it from
// the bare call in `subject_module` and reports 0 diagnostics.
//
// NOTHING ENROLLED EXECUTES THESE THREE ARMS, AND THAT IS STATED HERE SO NOBODY CITES THEM AS
// COVERAGE. The command below is the instrument and the outcome beside it is what that command
// must produce -- a specification to re-derive, never a receipt, because DESIGN section 4c says an
// annotation can be evidence for nothing a machine claims.
//
// THE HARNESS THAT WOULD RUN THEM DOES NOT EXIST YET, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. The corpus's
// one nested-compile surface, `gunbc.compile_diagnostic_census` `compile_dag_diagnostic_census`,
// resolves ONLY a probe source's authored import closure: a bare call to a corpus function from an
// import-less probe refuses there with `InternalError function:<name>`, measured on two definers in
// two different source roots. So the ACCEPTING arm of this discriminator is unauthorable in it --
// the one harness available is blind to precisely the manufactured edge this fixture is about, which
// is the same asymmetry one level up in the instrument. Under DESIGN section 4b that missing harness
// is this class's next-rung trigger, not a permanent ceiling: a nested compile that derives the
// closure the way the real one does would run all three arms unchanged. `if_join_pool_binding`
// stands the same way and for the same reason.
//
//   gunbc compile --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 \
//     --source-root fixtures/free_call_pool_binding \
//     --entry fixtures/free_call_pool_binding/closure_without_definer.dag --target dag
import fixtures.free_call_pool_binding.subject_module { subject_use }

fn plain() -> Int {
  subject_use()
}
